Target: Asterinas 604948581512d83734377974d4c34adb4530f2d7

Ramfs: zero-count positional write must not enlarge the file

RamInode::write_at publishes new_size = offset + write_len whenever it
exceeds the current size, with no special case for write_len == 0. A
pwrite64(fd, off > EOF, 0) therefore grows inode_meta.size and the page
cache while copying zero bytes and returning Ok(0).

POSIX write(2) requires an nbyte == 0 write to a regular file to "return
zero and have no other results"; Linux (tmpfs, ext4) honors this. ext2 in
the same tree already guards (ext2/inode/file.rs:45). Mirror that guard,
placed before the mtime/ctime update so a zero-length write has no
observable effect at all.

Reproduced and fix-verified with repro/test_bugMC-6_run.sh (guest
test_bugMC-6_ramfs_empty_pwrite.c): unpatched kernel reports
MC6_RESULT BUG_TRIGGERED, patched kernel reports MC6_RESULT OK.
---
 kernel/core/src/fs/fs_impls/ramfs/fs.rs | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/kernel/core/src/fs/fs_impls/ramfs/fs.rs
+++ b/kernel/core/src/fs/fs_impls/ramfs/fs.rs
@@ -732,7 +732,10 @@
 
                 let mut inode_meta = self.metadata.lock();
                 let file_size = inode_meta.size;
                 let write_len = reader.remain();
+                if write_len == 0 {
+                    return Ok(0);
+                }
                 let new_size = offset + write_len;
                 let should_expand_size = new_size > file_size;
                 let new_size_aligned = new_size.align_up(BLOCK_SIZE);
